<task id="122" project="unified-access-control-r2">
  <metadata>
    <title>Segregate the Foundry agent thread per conversation — the Redis key that decides which conversation you rejoin is a confidentiality boundary, and both of its segments are compile-time constants</title>
    <phase>Register-driven (authored 2026-09-29, session 25; D-12 item 6 + ADR-009 subject-discrimination MUST)</phase>
    <gate>startable</gate>
    <status>completed</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Modifies .cs on an AI conversation path and changes a cache key that currently leaks conversation content between users. Tags include auth.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Security-sensitive, four call sites with four different available discriminators, and one caller whose stated intent (ephemeral) contradicts its implementation (resume-from-cache).</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Signature change on AgentServiceClient fans out to four callers across Chat, Nodes, Handlers and Foundry.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>none</deps>
    <tags>bff-api, auth, ai, cache, testing</tags>
    <estimated-effort>4-6 hours</estimated-effort>
    <origin>D-12 item 6 / ADR-009 "MUST (added 2026-09-28): the key part AFTER the tenant segment MUST discriminate the subject". Owner decision 2026-09-29: "It has to be fully segregated -- per tenant, per user, per conversation/session."</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>A Foundry thread is a server-side conversation: SendMessageAsync appends to it and the agent replies with the whole accumulated thread as context. Redis stores which thread to rejoin. Today that key names only the tenant — so two people in the same company, chatting within an hour, rejoin the SAME conversation. Make the key name the conversation.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer — the AI chat pipeline and the Foundry agent client. You are changing what a cached identifier means; get it wrong in the loose direction and conversations merge across users.</role>

  <goal>Every cached Foundry thread is scoped to ONE conversation: the chat path by session, the playbook path by run, the legal-research path by its chat session. The code-interpreter path stops caching entirely, which is what its own comment already claims. No caller passes a compile-time constant where an identity belongs, and a test fails if any of them regresses.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>🔴 WHAT "FOUNDRY" MEANS HERE, because the repo contains three agent stacks and only one is this:
  - `Azure.AI.Projects` 1.0.0-beta.8 → **Azure AI Foundry Agent Service** (`AgentsClient`, `CreateThreadAsync` / `CreateMessageAsync` / `CreateRunStreamingAsync`). THIS is the subject. Used by `AgentServiceClient` and `CodeInterpreterBridge`.
  - `Microsoft.Agents.Builder` / `.Core.Models` → **Microsoft 365 Agents SDK**, used ONLY by `Api/Agent/SpaarkeAgentHandler.cs` (the Copilot/Teams channel). Not this.
  - `Microsoft.Agents.AI` 1.0.0-rc1 → **Microsoft Agent Framework**. Referenced in the csproj, ZERO call sites (`AiModule.cs:93` claims `chatClient.AsAIAgent()` is used by SprkChatAgentFactory — it is not). Not this, and separately worth retiring.
It is NOT Foundry IQ (a knowledge/retrieval product) and NOT Foundry Local.

🔴 THE DEFECT. `TenantCache.BuildKey` (`Infrastructure/Cache/TenantCache.cs:258`) is
`tenant:{tenantId}:{resource}:{id}:v{version}`. `AgentServiceClient` passes resource=`agent-thread`,
id=`thread` — BOTH compile-time constants (`AgentServiceClient.cs:54-55`). So the whole key varies by the
first argument alone. TTL 60 min, refreshed on every access (`AgentServiceOptions.ThreadCacheExpiryMinutes`).

⚠️ AND THE FIRST ARGUMENT IS NOT ALWAYS A TENANT. It lands in the tenant slot, and two of four callers pass a
literal:

| Caller | line | first arg | effect |
|---|---|---|---|
| `Chat/Middleware/AgentServiceRoutingMiddleware.cs` | :376 | `_tenantId` | one thread per tenant, shared by every user in it |
| `Services/Ai/Nodes/AgentServiceNodeExecutor.cs` | :170 | `config.TenantId` | same |
| `Services/Ai/Handlers/LegalResearchHandler.cs` | :571 | 🔴 `"legal-research-grounding"` | ONE GLOBAL THREAD across every tenant and user |
| `Services/Ai/Foundry/CodeInterpreterBridge.cs` | :106 | 🔴 `"_code_interpreter_ephemeral_"` | same — and its comment says "ephemeral … stateless sandbox per call" while calling CreateOrResume, which CACHES AND RESUMES. Intent and implementation disagree. |

🔴 `tenantId` IS NOT A CUSTOMER DISCRIMINATOR (D-12). Every Model 1 customer presents Spaarke's tenantId, so
the tenant segment separates nothing between customers and its tests pass anyway. **Customer separation is
the dedicated per-customer Redis INSTANCE** (ADR-009 as amended; access control is per-instance, not
per-keyspace). Do NOT try to fix customer separation in the key — see [D-14](../notes/D-14-customer-discriminator.md), which is task 123.
This task fixes the two axes that ARE the key's job: **user** and **conversation**.

✅ THE DISCRIMINATORS ALREADY EXIST — none needs inventing (verified 2026-09-29):
  - Chat: `SprkChatAgentFactory.CreateAgentAsync` takes `sessionId` (`:367`) one line from `tenantId` (`:370`) and simply never passes it to the middleware, which is constructed with tenantId only (`:1159-1164`, ctor `:175-187`). `ChatContext` carries NO user or session, so the value must come from the factory.
  - Playbook: `NodeExecutionContext.RunId` — `required Guid` (`Services/Ai/NodeExecutionContext.cs:30`).
  - Legal research: `ChatInvocationContext.ChatSessionId`, already used for the correlation log id at `LegalResearchHandler.cs:295`.
  - Code interpreter: nothing needed — it must stop caching.

💡 WHY SESSION IS SUFFICIENT, AND USER IS NOT SEPARATELY REQUIRED. A chat session belongs to exactly one
user, so session-keying subsumes user-keying for the leak AND additionally stops one user's unrelated
questions sharing context (ask about Matter X, then Matter Y forty minutes later — X is still in the
agent's context today). Keying by user alone would fix the leak and leave that.

⚠️ LATENT, NOT LIVE. `AgentServiceOptions.Enabled` defaults **false** (`AgentServiceOptions.cs:26`) and
`GuardEnabled()` throws before any cache access (`AgentServiceClient.cs:115`); no config sets it true. So no
thread is created or resumed today. That makes this the cheap moment — the same reasoning that made the
`Secure Record` rename cheap — and it means there is no cache to migrate.

⚠️ THE OTHER TWO SITES IN THE ORIGINAL ITEM-6 LIST ARE **NOT** VIOLATIONS. The MUST is conditional —
"whenever the cached value differs per principal". Verified:
  - `AgentConfigurationService` (`agent-config:exposed-playbooks`, `:capabilities`) caches TENANT-WIDE admin
    config; neither cached method takes a principal, and `IsRolePermittedAsync` (the only role-aware one)
    reads options WITHOUT caching. Not a violation.
  - `ModuleEntitlementResolver` (`approle-module-map:all`) caches the GLOBAL map as DATA; the per-user step
    (`App-Role claims ∩ map`) runs after the read, per request. Not a violation.
🔴 **DO NOT "fix" either.** Adding a user segment would fragment a tenant-global cache per user, multiplying
Redis entries and misses for zero security benefit. The earlier handoff note claiming all three failed was
wrong; task 123's note records why.</background>
